# the site host's environment, and the only file it needs. the service serves model-authored pages to
# the internet, so it never receives the bot's file: no API keys, no Telegram token, nothing an escape
# could reuse. the publishing secret below is the one thing it holds, and nginx, which reads this
# file too, gets even that blanked.
#
# copy this file to `.env` beside the compose file: into the deployment directory on a machine of its
# own, or to services/sites/.env beside the bot (docs/sites.md#both-on-one-machine). it configures the
# host the same way in either, and compose reads it twice — for the values it substitutes into the
# deployment, and as both containers' environment — with no flag of its own:
#   docker compose up -d

# required. the domain sites are served under: a person's site lands on <their id>.<this domain>, and
# the publishing API on api.<this domain>. both names must be proxied `A` records pointing here.
SITES_DOMAIN=

# where published sites live, relative to the compose file that mounts it. create it yourself
# before the first start — `mkdir -p data` — because a bind mount docker creates comes out owned by
# root, and the service runs as uid 1000. on a machine whose login user is uid 1000, that is the whole
# setup: no sudo, no chown.
# it is a plain directory on purpose: taking a page down is `rm -rf data/sites/<label>`, which has to
# work when the bot is not around.
# SITES_HOST_DIR=./data

# the directory holding three files, relative to the compose file that mounts it:
#   site.pem, site.key      a certificate covering the domain's wildcard, Cloudflare Origin CA or not
#   origin-pull-ca.pem      Cloudflare's Authenticated Origin Pulls CA; not needed with pulls off
# SITES_CERT_DIR=./certs

# required. the same secret the bot is given: generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`
SITES_TOKEN=
# or keep it in a file the runtime can read — /run, /data or /tmp — and name it here instead
# SITES_TOKEN_FILE=/run/secrets/sites-token

# everything below has a working default; see docs/sites.md.

# `off` where Cloudflare is not in front of this host: Authenticated Origin Pulls asks every caller for
# Cloudflare's own client certificate, so with anything else in front nginx refuses every request.
# with it off, `origin-pull-ca.pem` is not needed either.
# SITES_ORIGIN_PULLS=on

# the two images, both on the same tag: they are one deployment, and a template that does not match
# the service it fronts is a confusing failure.
# SITES_IMAGE=ghcr.io/helltar/vusan-sites:latest
# SITES_NGINX_IMAGE=ghcr.io/helltar/vusan-sites-nginx:latest

# one site's ceiling, and one file's. raising SITES_MAX_FILE_MB past nginx's own 32 MB body limit needs
# `client_max_body_size` in services/sites/nginx/default.conf.template raised with it.
# SITES_MAX_MB=100
# SITES_MAX_FILE_MB=25
# SITES_MAX_FILES=1000
# SITES_MAX_DEPTH=10

# nothing expires by default: a published site is a finished thing someone handed a link to, and the
# clock would only count republishing anyway — visits are served by nginx and never reach the service.
# set a number of days to delete a site nobody has republished for that long, or `never` to turn it
# back off without removing the line.
# SITES_RETAIN_DAYS=180
# publishing stops while the host has less than this much free space, and resumes on its own
# SITES_MIN_FREE_MB=2048
# SITES_PUBLISHES_PER_HOUR=20
# an upload nobody finished is discarded after this long
# SITES_UPLOAD_IDLE_MINUTES=15
